 "caption_words": 138,
 "caption_text": "Nobody crashed. Nobody merged. So why did all this traffic just stop? Get to the front, and there's nothing there. No accident. No roadwork. In a 2008 study, researchers in Japan put 22 cars on a 230-metre circular track, and asked every driver to cruise at about 30 kilometres an hour. The smooth flow didn't last. When one driver eases off a little, the driver behind reacts a moment late and brakes a little harder. The next driver brakes harder still. Those tiny delays grow into a stop-and-go wave that travels backwards, against the traffic. On real highways, waves like this drift backwards at roughly fifteen to twenty kilometres an hour. Later experiments found a single smoothly driven car could dampen these waves. So leave a little extra space. You might be the one who smooths it out.",
